    "body": "AI-generated image\nBy Alex Lennane\n18 November 2026\nEU imports of low-value ecommerce goods fell 63% year on year in the quarter immediately after the bloc introduced its €3 customs duty in July.\nNow, with an added €2 handling fee in force since 1 November, Seko Logistics is warning UK retailers that some Black Friday bundles could incur €15 in customs charges – before VAT.\nThe EC’s preliminary assessment counted 555 million low-value ecommerce items entering the EU in Q3, down from 1.51 billion a year earlier, and said some goods appeared to be coming in as bulk shipments for storage and fulfilment within the bloc. The figures cover the period before the 1 November fee.\nThis year’s Black Friday, on 27 November, will be the first since the EU removed its duty exemption for low-value parcels. Where both charges apply, each distinct line on the customs declaration adds €5. A jumper, candle, and mug declared on three lines would incur €9 in duty and €6 in handling fees, before VAT is added.\n“The retailers that will feel it most are the ones whose best-selling deals are bundles,” said Steve Aylott, Seko’s UK and Ireland sales director.\n“Five euros per product type sounds small, until it is sitting on a £20 Black Friday deal,” he added. “Retailers have to decide whether to absorb it, build it into European prices, or rethink the bundle. What they can’t afford is a customer finding out after they’ve paid.”\nThe origin of the goods can also change the bill. Goods that qualify for UK ‘preferential treatment’ can avoid the €3 duty if they are entered on a full customs declaration and VAT was collected outside the EU’s Import One-Stop Shop. The €2 handling fee still applies, and is non-refundable if the goods are returned.\n“Where a parcel ships from and where the goods come from are two different questions,” said Tim Harding, Seko’s UK and Ireland customs manager. “Customs looks at origin and classification. If the product data behind a Black Friday order is wrong, the duty will be wrong too, and peak is the worst possible time to find out.”\nAir cargo gateways are already seeing a change in the mix. Liège Airport said small-parcel volumes fell 42% after the July duty took effect, reducing the number of dedicated ecommerce flights. Total Q3 cargo tonnage was virtually flat year on year, at 334,467 tonnes.\nWorldACD said Hong Kong-Europe air cargo chargeable weight, across all commodities, was still down 32% year on year in the week of 2–8 November.\nAt TIACA’s Air Cargo Forum in Miami in October, Rotate’s Ryan Keyrouse identified Liège and Budapest as European ecommerce gateways hit by falling demand. Across the affected Europe-bound traffic, other commodities had not replaced the lost ecommerce volumes as they had following changes to US de minimis rules, he said.\n“You see that no other commodity picked up to backfill that,” he said. Airlines had, instead, redirected capacity from Europe towards North America and intra-Asia markets.\nFor retailers with steady EU demand, Seko said holding stock inside the bloc could make sense, allowing them to import in bulk and fulfil orders locally. Shein, which opened its Wrocław logistics hub in December 2025, said this month it was investing €500m this year to expand the operation.\nMeanwhile, the decline could spread further: the UK government plans to scrap customs duty relief on imports worth £135 or less by October 2028, six months earlier than originally planned.\nGoods will become subject to standard UK tariff rates, and the government plans a separate fee to cover the cost of processing low-value imports. Ministers have yet to set the fee, or decide whether to charge it per parcel or per item. A House of Lords subcommittee took evidence on the reforms last week and is due to hear from Treasury minister James Murray and HMRC officials tomorrow.\nElsewhere, J&T Express reported a record 136m parcels across its global network on 12 November, the day after China’s Double 11 shopping event. It averaged 119m parcels a day from 1 to 12 November, up 26% year on year.",
